from pathlib import Path
import html

out=Path('outputs/pca-studio')
rows=[
('What is PCA?','Rotate one line through a measured cloud. Watch four observations become four scores.','PCA finds new perpendicular axes, ordered by variance. Keeping fewer axes reduces the number of values per observation.'),
('One number is a position on a line','Select an observation. Link its point, projection, and single stored score.','The point and its score refer to the same observation.'),
('Some views lose more than others','Compare 45° and 135° with the same scale in both score plots.','A useful projection keeps a large part of the original squared spread.'),
('A projection follows a perpendicular','Move one point to its closest position on the chosen line.','The line is the axis that we KEEP; the viewing direction is perpendicular to it.'),
('What does one score lose?','Hide the original points. Reveal the possible points that share a score.','A score cannot recover movement across its axis.'),
('Turn the axes, then remove one','Switch between two rotated coordinates and one retained coordinate.','Rotation alone loses nothing. Removing a coordinate can lose detail.'),
('What will best mean?','Compare kept squared spread with squared reconstruction error.','These are two equivalent PCA objectives under the same Euclidean scale.'),
('See the whole method once','Step through center → find axes → keep scores → reconstruct.','The next chapters explain how to compute this result.'),
('Measure change from a shared center','Translate the cloud without changing its shape.','The PCA direction describes variation around the mean.'),
('Find the center of this cloud','Highlight one column and calculate its mean.','Introduce the mean only because we need this center.'),
('Move the center to zero','Scrub the translation. Keep observation labels fixed.','Subtract the corresponding feature mean from each observation.'),
('The distances do not change','Select two observations before and after translation.','Centering changes the origin, not the relative positions.'),
('Describe the line with two numbers','Rotate a unit arrow. Its horizontal and vertical parts update.','The vector w gives a direction and has length one.'),
('Calculate one score','Select A. Multiply each centered coordinate by its direction weight.','The dot product gives signed distance along a unit axis.'),
('Use one rule for every observation','Link each row of Xc to its score in Xc w.','Rows are observations; columns are features. Show the matrix shapes here.'),
('Keep the ruler length fixed','Double the direction length without changing its angle.','Without the unit constraint, scores and variance can grow without limit.'),
('How spread out are the scores?','Compare scores around zero at two angles.','Centered input produces scores whose mean is zero.'),
('Signs cancel; squares do not','Switch signed contributions to square areas.','Squaring measures both directions and supports the distance identity used later.'),
('Give spread a number','Add four squared scores and divide by three.','Use sample variance throughout. An optional note explains n−1; changing it to n does not change the best axis.'),
('Search with a variance curve','Rotate the line. Link the angle to a point on the variance curve.','The highest point gives our numerical target. We still need a general method.'),
('Open one squared score','Reveal each term in (w1 a + w2 b)².','Show the two identical cross-products before replacing them with a factor of two.'),
('Add the same terms across rows','Select one observation in the sum. Then group matching terms.','The weights are shared by all observations, so they can leave the sum.'),
('Two familiar sums and one new sum','Link the first and last terms to the two feature variances.','The middle sum measures how the two centered features vary together.'),
('Build covariance from signed products','Select observations in each quadrant. Flip the second feature.','Same signs give positive products; opposite signs give negative products.'),
('Variance alone cannot tell the tilt','Compare two clouds with the same marginal variances and opposite covariance.','The cross-term distinguishes these clouds.'),
('Store the three quantities in S','Select a covariance matrix entry to reveal its products.','The diagonal stores variances. Both off-diagonal entries store the same covariance.'),
('Compute S with one matrix product','Animate a selected row of Xcᵀ meeting a column of Xc.','Transpose and multiplication are compact ways to do the sums we already used.'),
('Read wᵀ S w from inside out','Calculate Sw, then the dot product with w.','The compact formula is exactly the expanded variance expression.'),
('Check both routes with real numbers','Compare direct squared scores with wᵀ S w at any angle.','The two calculations agree because we derived their identity.'),
('State the optimization problem','Show a unit circle of permitted directions and a variance curve.','Maximize wᵀ S w with wᵀ w = 1.'),
('What does S do to an arrow?','Move w around the unit circle. Show Sw on a common labeled scale.','A matrix is also a rule that maps one vector to another.'),
('At an optimum, turning cannot help','Compare a direction with small clockwise and counterclockwise turns.','The allowed change runs along the circle. At an optimum its first-order gain is zero.'),
('Find the variance slope','Differentiate the two-coordinate quadratic one term at a time.','The gradient is (2S11w1+2S12w2, 2S12w1+2S22w2) = 2Sw.'),
('Find the constraint direction','Differentiate w1²+w2². Show the normal arrow to the circle.','The gradient 2w points outward; allowed small changes point perpendicular to it.'),
('Match the two normal directions','Align the gradient arrows at a stationary point.','2Sw = 2λw, so Sw = λw. This finds candidates; it does not yet prove a maximum.'),
('Name the equation after it appears','Compare w and Sw at both stationary directions.','An eigenvector keeps its direction under S. Its eigenvalue is the scale factor.'),
('Find the possible scale factors','Subtract λ on the diagonal. Expand the 2×2 determinant.','A nonzero solution requires S−λI to be singular. Explain determinant only here.'),
('Solve our two eigenvalues','Factor (10/3−λ)²−(8/3)². Link each root to the curve.','The roots are 6 and 2/3.'),
('Find and normalize the directions','Solve w2=w1 and w2=−w1. Set each vector length to one.','The directions are (1,1)/√2 and (1,−1)/√2.'),
('A candidate can be a minimum','Select each eigenvector and compare its variance.','The eigenvector equation alone does not choose PC1.'),
('Why are these directions perpendicular?','Expand q1ᵀSq2 in two ways and compare.','Symmetry gives (λ1−λ2)q1ᵀq2=0. Distinct eigenvalues imply perpendicular eigenvectors.'),
('Build any unit direction from them','Rotate w and show coefficients a and b on the two eigenvectors.','w=aq1+bq2 and a²+b²=1. State the symmetric-matrix basis theorem for higher dimensions.'),
('Expand variance in the new basis','Open four terms. Cancel both cross-terms with orthogonality.','The result is λ1a²+λ2b².'),
('Prove the largest value wins','Move the squared-weight allocation between λ1 and λ2.','A weighted average cannot exceed its largest entry. Equality at q1 proves global optimality.'),
('What if the eigenvalues tie?','Change to an isotropic cloud. Rotate freely.','All directions in a tied eigenspace give the same variance. A unique PC1 is not guaranteed.'),
('Find the next component','Restrict the next direction to the perpendicular space.','Maximize remaining variance there. In two dimensions the remaining axis is fixed up to sign.'),
('Read the PCA coordinates','Rotate both axes, then relabel the plot as PC1 and PC2.','Loadings describe axes. Scores describe observations.'),
('Check the new covariance','Select an entry of QᵀSQ. See the off-diagonal product sum vanish.','The score columns are uncorrelated. This does not generally mean independent.'),
('Explain the 90 percent','Connect eigenvalue bars to the sum of squared centered distances.','6/(6+2/3)=90%. This is a fraction of variance, not prediction accuracy.'),
('Rebuild an observation from its score','Select A. Multiply its score by q1, then add the mean.','The reconstructed point lies on the retained line.'),
('Draw the missing part','Show the original vector, projection, and residual as a right triangle.','Orthogonality gives original squared length = kept squared length + lost squared length.'),
('Prove the second objective','Add the triangle identity across all observations.','Total squared length is fixed, so maximizing kept variance minimizes squared reconstruction error.'),
('Recover both coordinates','Switch between k=0, k=1, and k=2.','Two orthonormal coordinates recover the original centered vector.'),
('Choose how much to keep','Set a retained-variance target and select k.','The task determines an acceptable error. A percentage alone does not prove usefulness.'),
('Extend from two features to p','Keep the top k eigenvalues in a larger spectrum.','For orthonormal W, retained variance is tr(WᵀSW). The top k eigenvectors maximize it; discarded eigenvalues give the loss.'),
('Connect the result to SVD','Substitute Xc=UΣVᵀ into S=XcᵀXc/(n−1).','The columns of V are PCA directions. Eigenvalues equal squared singular values divided by n−1.'),
('Use the axes on a new observation','Enter a new observation and apply the training mean and axes.','Fit preprocessing and PCA on training data. Reuse those values for later observations.'),
('Decide what units mean','Change one feature scale, then compare standardization.','Standardization changes the question. It gives each nonconstant feature unit variance.'),
('Large variance is not always useful','Reveal a distant observation and then category labels.','Outliers can dominate squared spread. A useful label can lie in a low-variance direction.'),
('A line cannot follow every shape','Compare a ring with the elongated cloud.','PCA is linear. Two components may be necessary even if a curve has one intrinsic parameter.'),
('Change the real notebook data','Use native marimo controls to change an observation.','Python recomputes mean, covariance, directions, scores, and reconstruction.'),
('Solve a fresh geometric problem','Choose a direction for a rotated, unseen cloud before seeing its optimum.','Require a prediction and a reason; then compare with the computed result.'),
('Produce the calculation yourself','Compute a covariance entry, a score, and a reconstruction for new values.','Record numeric correctness separately from the learner’s written explanation.'),
('Teach PCA back in your own words','Explain purpose → objective → covariance → eigenvectors → loss.','Use new examples and name one limit. Reopen the exact relevant scene after a miss.'),
]
assert len(rows)==64
acts=[('1–8','What does PCA do?','12–18 min'),('9–20','How do we measure a view?','20–30 min'),('21–30','Why does covariance appear?','20–30 min'),('31–45','Why do eigenvectors solve it?','30–45 min'),('46–56','What can we keep and recover?','25–35 min'),('57–64','When does the result help?','20–30 min')]
head='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>PCA teaching strategy</title><style>body{margin:0;background:#f5f2e9;color:#17243d;font:18px/1.65 system-ui}main{max-width:1100px;margin:auto;padding:60px 28px}h1{font:64px/1.05 Georgia;max-width:900px}h2{margin-top:48px;font-size:28px}p{max-width:900px}a{color:#166d71}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}th,td{padding:16px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #c9c9bf;vertical-align:top}th{font-size:14px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em}small{color:#536174}details{padding:16px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #c9c9bf}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:650}strong{color:#135e66}.eyebrow{letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:13px}blockquote{border-left:4px solid #168889;padding-left:22px}code{background:#e5e9e4;padding:2px 5px}.status{background:#fff;padding:20px;border-left:5px solid #cd7848}</style><main>'''
body='''<p class="eyebrow">Teaching strategy · revision 2 · 4 October 2026</p><h1>Meet PCA first.<br>Derive the method from the picture.</h1>
<p class="status">This is the redesign specification. It replaces the rejected prerequisite-first sequence. The slide count is a structure for the argument, not evidence of learning.</p>
<p>The learner first sees what PCA does. The learner then measures a projection and derives the method that chooses it. Each new mathematical tool answers a question that the picture has already raised.</p>
<h2>What the learner must be able to do</h2><p>Choose a useful projection, calculate its variance, derive the covariance objective, explain why the largest eigenvalue solves it, reconstruct an observation, and identify a limit of the method. The final check uses new data.</p>
<h2>Six questions, one route</h2><p>The map shows these six questions in order, the current position, and direct return links. It does not show an intimidating tree of prerequisite topics. Estimated time includes experiments and practice; there is no timed lock.</p><table><tr><th>Slides</th><th>Question</th><th>Estimated learning time</th></tr>'''
body+=''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(v)+'</td>' for v in a)+'</tr>' for a in acts)+'</table>'
body+='''<h2>Keep the proof in the main route</h2><p>Slides 21–45 carry the main derivation. First expand a squared score. The cross-term creates the need for covariance. Collect the terms into S. Derive the stationary equation Sw=λw. Then prove global optimality using a weighted average in an orthonormal eigenbasis. Do not present stationarity as proof of a maximum.</p><p>Slides 50–52 derive reconstruction and the equivalent minimum-error objective. Slides 55–56 explain the extension to several retained components and the relation to SVD. The symmetric-matrix basis theorem is stated as a theorem; the lesson does not pretend to prove all of spectral theory.</p>
<h2>Introduce the math at its point of use</h2><p>The mean appears when the cloud needs a center. A vector appears when the line needs a direction. A dot product appears when a point needs a score. Matrix multiplication appears after the individual sums are clear. Gradients and determinants appear inside the PCA proof.</p><p>Each refresher opens beside the current scene. It returns to the same angle, selected observation, and proof step. There is no separate compulsory algebra course before PCA.</p>
<h2>A stable example and stable notation</h2><p>Use four invented position measurements in centimeters: A=(8,7), B=(7,8), C=(5,4), D=(4,5). The center is (6,6). Centered rows are (2,1), (1,2), (−1,−2), (−2,−1). Their covariance is [[10,8],[8,10]]/3. The eigenvalues are 6 and 2/3.</p><p>Use Xc for the centered data table, xᵢ for one centered observation, w for a candidate unit direction, zᵢ for its score, S for covariance, and qⱼ for a learned eigenvector. Keep observation rows and feature columns consistent. A and B share a PC1 score; show that loss explicitly. Do not claim that PCA preserves every pairwise distance or every distinction.</p>
<h2>Visual and interaction design</h2><p>Make the geometric illustration the largest region. Use one dark ink color, teal for the retained direction, and warm orange for the residual. Keep scales equal. Put labels beside the object they describe. Show only the quantities needed for the current claim.</p><p>Projection scenes link the cloud, a separate score line, and retained/lost spread. Covariance scenes link colored products to one selected matrix entry. Proof scenes keep a small live geometric witness beside one algebra step. Reconstruction scenes join the score to the rebuilt point and its residual.</p><p>Controls must change an explanatory quantity. A reveal button only counts as an interaction when it exposes a reason or a learner-tested prediction. Provide keyboard alternatives for every pointer control. Pause motion by default and respect reduced-motion settings.</p>
<h2>Plain technical English</h2><p>Target at least 80% of learner-facing prose sentences for compatibility with an ASD-STE100-based review profile. Count sentences, not just short words. Review active voice, one subject, consistent technical names, approved word meaning, and sentence length. Use at most 20 words in instructions and 25 in descriptions. Define necessary mathematical terms in a course glossary.</p><p>Record vocabulary exceptions. A sentence-length script is only one check; it cannot certify dictionary or meaning compliance. Do not claim a measured 80% compatibility rate until the vocabulary and usage review has been completed.</p>
<h2>Use native marimo-studio</h2><p>Keep the live computation in the Python notebook. Use the native Reveal.js Studio view for the slides, native marimo controls for data changes, and named projections for results. Keep fast local angle selection for immediate visual feedback. Give every slide a visible Back to notebook action. Test the action from both the editor preview and the standalone slide view.</p>
<h2>Research that informs this design</h2><p>I read the English transcripts and chapter lists of <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g-Hb26agBFg">Luis Serrano’s PCA lesson</a> and <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FgakZw6K1QQ">StatQuest’s step-by-step PCA lesson</a>. Serrano establishes projection before the statistics. StatQuest uses a right triangle to connect retained and lost squared distance. This course uses original scenes and adds an explicit covariance optimization proof.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.1100">Shlens’s PCA tutorial</a> supports the geometry, covariance, and SVD connections. Our data convention uses observations in rows. <a href="https://www.asd-ste100.org/assets/files/ASD-STE100_ISSUE9.pdf">ASD-STE100 Issue 9</a> supplies the writing reference. The user’s AI Learn Loop guidance informs prediction before feedback and separate checks of calculation and explanation.</p>
<h2>Acceptance checks</h2><ol><li>By slide 8, the learner has seen PCA, compression, reconstruction, and the intended objective.</li><li>Every main derivation step gives its reason and defines any new symbol.</li><li>Covariance arithmetic, eigenpairs, score variance, and reconstruction agree with NumPy.</li><li>Stationary directions include both the maximum and minimum. The global proof selects the correct one.</li><li>One complete path works with the keyboard. Controls update both the picture and the numbers.</li><li>The notebook return action works. Refresh does not leave an invisible or mismatched slide.</li><li>Inspect representative geometry, covariance, proof, and reconstruction slides at desktop and narrow sizes.</li><li>Record language checks honestly. Do not equate passing a numerical quiz with understanding the proof.</li></ol>
<h2>64-slide storyboard</h2><p>Each item states the scene and the teaching claim. A slide is one step in the same argument.</p>'''
body+=''.join(f'<details><summary>{i:02d} · {html.escape(t)}</summary><p><strong>Scene:</strong> {html.escape(v)}</p><p><strong>Claim:</strong> {html.escape(c)}</p></details>' for i,(t,v,c) in enumerate(rows,1))
(out/'PCA-TEACHING-PLAN.html').write_text(head+body+'</main></html>',encoding='utf-8')
import json
Path('work/redesign_storyboard.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
print('Saved the 64-slide teaching strategy and storyboard.')
